=== CORRECTION, written 2026-08-30T12:29Z, before any measurement ===
The "BUILD DID NOT CHANGE THE BINARY" line above is MY ERROR, not a finding.
Sequence of what actually happened:
  1. 12:25:34Z a setsid'd `ninja -C build -j10 ndtwin_kernel` was started. It ran SIX steps
     (see [1/6]..[6/6] above), including relinking bin/ndtwin_kernel, against HEAD 1208d22.
  2. I read the log ~3 s in, saw only "Entering directory", and wrongly concluded the detached
     build had died. (Reading a log once and concluding from absence -- H-24, again.)
  3. I then took PRE=sha256 -- which was already the POST value of step 1 -- and re-ran ninja,
     which correctly said "no work to do". PRE==POST followed by construction.
What the evidence actually supports, and it is the stronger statement:
  * A full rebuild from HEAD 1208d22's source at 12:25Z produced a binary BYTE-IDENTICAL to the
    one dated 14:01 local. Determinism aside, that means the 14:01 build already contained every
    source change through 87d272f (T-7b release_lock/renew_lock).
  * The three source files whose mtime was NEWER than the binary
    (src/ndt_core/http/HttpSession.cpp, include/.../LockManager.hpp, include/.../HttpSession.hpp)
    differ from HEAD by nothing -- `git diff HEAD -- <them>` is empty. Their mtimes were bumped by
    the branch-surgery re-checkout, not by an unbuilt edit.
  * So: mtime said "3 files are newer than the binary" and the honest reading of that was
    "possibly stale". Content says "not stale". memory: benchmark-must-name-the-binary-it-measured
    -- mtime does not even give a lower bound. This round's binary is identified by sha256 and by
    a from-source rebuild, never by date.
